1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,581
Independent living is often lost in the
space between “everything is fine”

2
00:00:09,661 --> 00:00:11,461
and an emergency:

3
00:00:11,541 --> 00:00:13,341
one missed check-in,

4
00:00:13,421 --> 00:00:15,282
one small household problem,

5
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:18,153
or one favor nobody clearly owns.

6
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:22,884
STAY gives Sarah one calm place for that
middle ground.

7
00:00:22,964 --> 00:00:29,475
Today shows one thing at a time, an ordinary
home check, and useful context.

8
00:00:29,555 --> 00:00:36,997
Every weather, device, utility, maintenance, and travel signal
in this demo is clearly labeled as simulated.

9
00:00:37,077 --> 00:00:41,728
Access preferences make the same experience
work voice-first or touch-first.

10
00:00:41,808 --> 00:00:48,785
Sarah can choose captions, more response time, higher
contrast, reduced motion, and repeated key information.

11
00:00:48,865 --> 00:00:50,726
These settings change presentation.

12
00:00:50,806 --> 00:00:53,596
They never change her safety policy.

13
00:00:53,676 --> 00:00:56,932
Privacy is scoped by role and moment.

14
00:00:57,012 --> 00:00:58,873
Routine details stay private.

15
00:00:58,953 --> 00:01:06,860
Access instructions and location can appear only inside an
authenticated, assigned incident when Sarah’s plan permits it.

16
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:10,195
Now Sarah misses her Morning Safety Window.

17
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:16,787
STAY makes one deterministic check, waits through
the grace period, and checks once more.

18
00:01:16,867 --> 00:01:20,123
Bedrock does not choose what happens next.

19
00:01:20,203 --> 00:01:22,063
Sarah’s saved plan does.

20
00:01:22,143 --> 00:01:27,260
After the second missed check, her Circle
plan opens an incident.

21
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,130
STAY has not contacted emergency services.

22
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:33,001
Sarah asks Tom, a nearby helper.

23
00:01:33,081 --> 00:01:39,127
Only the minimum incident detail is shared,
and every action enters the timeline.

24
00:01:39,207 --> 00:01:41,007
Tom accepts.

25
00:01:41,087 --> 00:01:43,413
Tom is on the way.

26
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:49,074
He now owns the response, and the Circle
sees the same update.

27
00:01:49,154 --> 00:01:55,201
STAY also handles ordinary Help Requests, household
playbooks, availability, and privacy-filtered House Memory.

28
00:01:55,281 --> 00:02:00,397
The deterministic workflows keep working if
the language model is unavailable.

29
00:02:00,477 --> 00:02:02,277
For Alexa Plus,

30
00:02:02,357 --> 00:02:08,404
STAY exposes ten goal-level tools through a
standards-based MCP server with accessible text,

31
00:02:08,484 --> 00:02:10,284
typed structured content,

32
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:12,164
provenance,

33
00:02:12,244 --> 00:02:14,044
and Alexa-style widgets.

34
00:02:14,124 --> 00:02:15,924
On AWS,

35
00:02:16,004 --> 00:02:17,804
Cognito protects identity;

36
00:02:17,884 --> 00:02:19,684
DynamoDB transactions

37
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:22,089
and an outbox preserve state;

38
00:02:22,169 --> 00:02:23,969
Scheduler

39
00:02:24,049 --> 00:02:25,910
and EventBridge coordinate checks;

40
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:27,790
WebSockets

41
00:02:27,870 --> 00:02:30,195
and minimal email deliver updates;

42
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:32,601
and Nova interprets only redacted,

43
00:02:32,681 --> 00:02:34,481
non-critical intent.

44
00:02:34,561 --> 00:02:42,468
STAY does not contact emergency services, diagnose a condition,
detect a fall, or replace Alexa Emergency Assist.

45
00:02:42,548 --> 00:02:48,129
STAY helps people stay at home, stay
connected, and stay in control.

